nodes.goal:
Author an artifact that satisfies this rubric: {run.rubric}
Brief: {run.artifact_spec}
Write a self-contained markdown artifact, one file inside your own work dir, that answers the brief as well as the rubric allows. The path is the commitment - sibling seats see the file, never your work dir.
Return {"artifact": "<absolute path of the file you wrote>", "summary": "<what you wrote, two lines>"}

Score the artifact against the rubric verbatim. Rubric: {run.rubric}
The artifact's absolute path is in ## Inputs. Open it and read it whole before judging.
Return {"verdict": "pass|findings", "findings": ["<one per line, each naming the rubric criterion it violates>"]}. Use 'pass' only when nothing load-bearing is wrong; with 'findings' the list is never empty, and every concern you raise is a finding.

Address the rubric findings with EXACTLY ONE repair pass - the re-score that follows you is the last scoring this artifact gets before a disposition.
The findings ride in ## Inputs verbatim. Rewrite the artifact so every named finding is cleared; clear the findings, do not grow the scope. If you write a new file, commit its path.
Return {"artifact": "<absolute path of the repaired file>", "addressed": ["<each finding, restated verbatim>"]}

Re-score the repaired artifact against the rubric verbatim. Rubric: {run.rubric}
## Inputs carries the repaired artifact path and the original findings list. Open the artifact yourself; re-check every original finding against the file, and score the rubric afresh - a repair can fix one criterion and break another.
disagreements is the number of scoring rounds that found the artifact wanting: the first pass already counted its own, so it counts as round 1. If you too find it wanting after the one repair, set disagreements: 2 and the second-disagreement STOP trips; if the repair cleared everything, set disagreements: 1.
Return {"verdict": "pass|findings", "findings": ["<residual or newly-named>"], "disagreements": <number>}
